Seven Remarkable Facts About
Octopuses
A short illustrated-by-words guide to one of the ocean's strangest minds
1. Three hearts, blue blood
An octopus has three hearts: two pump blood through the gills, and one circulates it to the rest
of the body. Its blood is blue because it uses copper-based hemocyanin rather than iron-based
hemoglobin to carry oxygen.
2. Most of the neurons are in the arms
Roughly two thirds of an octopus's neurons sit in its arms rather than its central brain. Each
arm can explore, grab, and taste with a good deal of independence.
3. Masters of camouflage
Specialized skin cells called chromatophores let an octopus change color and pattern in a
fraction of a second. It can also alter skin texture to mimic rocks, coral, or seaweed.
4. Escape artists
With no bones, an octopus can squeeze through any opening larger than its beak, the only
hard part of its body. Aquarium keepers have long told stories of octopuses slipping out of
tanks.
5. Short, intense lives
Many species live only one to two years. Females typically guard their eggs without feeding
and die soon after they hatch.
6. Tool use
Veined octopuses have been observed carrying coconut shell halves to use later as portable
shelters, a rare example of tool use in invertebrates.
7. Problem solvers
In lab settings, octopuses unscrew jars, navigate mazes, and learn by observation. Their
intelligence evolved along a path completely separate from that of mammals and birds.
Quick Comparison
Trait Octopus Human
Hearts 3 1
Blood color Blue (copper-based) Red (iron-based)
Typical lifespan 1 to 2 years 70+ years
Skeleton None Internal bones